"Policy on Prioritizing Canadian Suppliers and Canadian Content in Strategic Federal Procurements | CanadaBuys (https://canadabuys.canada.ca/en/buy-canadian-policy/policy-prioritizing-canadian-suppliers-and-canadian-content-strategic-federal-procurements)\nciteturn32view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://canadabuys.canada.ca/en/buy-canadian-policy/policy-prioritizing-canadian-suppliers-and-canadian-content-strategic-federal-procurements\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 443\nL0: Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website.\nL1: \nL2:   * cite0†Skip to main content L3:   * cite1†Skip to \"About Canada.ca\" L4: \nL5: ## Language selection\nL6: \nL7:   * cite2†fr (Français) L8: \nL9: Search CanadaBuys [Input: Search CanadaBuys]\nL10: \nL11: Search\nL12: \nL13: ## Canada.ca Menu\nL14: \nL15: Main Menu\nL16: \nL17: ## Buy Canadian Policy\nL18: \nL19: The Government of Canada has introduced new policies to protect, build, and transform Canadian industries. Check out cite3†Buy Canadian Policy for more information on what this could mean for you.\nL20: \nL21: * * *\nL22: # Policy on Prioritizing Canadian Suppliers and Canadian Content in Strategic Federal Procurements\nL23: \nL24:   1. ## 1. Effective date\nL25:     1. The Policy on Prioritizing Canadian Suppliers and Canadian Content in Strategic Federal Procurements (hereafter, “the Policy”), issued pursuant to the Buy Canadian Procurement Policy Framework, takes effect on December 16, 2025.\nL26:     2. The Policy does not apply to solicitations published or contracts awarded prior to the effective date.\nL27:     3. For existing Supply Arrangements (SAs), including any solicitations issued from an existing SA (Supply Arrangements), the Policy applies at the time of the next renewal. Departments may choose to incorporate Canadian suppliers and Canadian content requirements earlier, provided these elements are reflected in the updated Request for Supply Arrangement (RFSA). For existing Standing Offers (SOs), the Policy applies at their next renewal.\nL28:   2. ## 2. Purpose\nL29:     1. The Policy advances the Government of Canada’s mission to build a strong and diversified Canadian economy. It encourages the participation of Canadian suppliers and incentivizes the use of Canadian content in strategic procurement.\nL30:     2. The Policy utilizes federal purchasing to advance Canada's economic, industrial, and innovation priorities. This includes strengthening domestic supply chains, supporting Canadian businesses, and enhancing national resilience.\nL31:     3. The Policy encourages the participation of Canadian suppliers by giving them a price-based evaluation credit during the offer evaluation process.\nL32:     4. The Policy incentivizes the use of Canadian content by evaluating offers based on the inclusion of Canadian goods and/or Canadian services and/or Canadian value-added, as defined in the Policy.\nL33:   3. ## 3. Definitions\nL34:     1. Strategic Procurement means:\nL35:        * procurement above the financial threshold set out in section 4.4 and that falls within one of the sectors and commodities listed in cite4†Appendix A . These sectors are prioritized because they contribute to risk mitigation, drive innovation, strengthen national security and reinforce Canada’s strong and resilient economy.\nL36:     2. Canadian Supplier means:\nL37:        * a supplier that has a place of business in Canada where it conducts activities on a permanent basis that is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours; or, a joint venture where each member of the joint venture has a place of business in Canada where it conducts activities on a permanent basis that is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours; and\nL38:          * is registered and files taxes in Canada (e.g., GST (Goods and Services Tax)/HST (Harmonized Sales Tax), corporate income tax);\nL39:          * maintains a registered address in Canada and employs personnel and/or conducts day-to-day business activities in Canada; and\nL40:          * will not subcontract work to non-Canadian suppliers or individuals located outside Canada, in a manner that results in minimal value-added activities being performed within Canada.\nL41:        * In assessing whether a supplier is Canadian, the contracting authority:\nL42:          * may consider the nature of the industry or sector, the geographical location of the supplier, and the level of activity typically performed by other suppliers competing in the same sector;\nL43:          * must provide the supplier with an opportunity to submit relevant evidence; and,\nL44:          * must clearly document the rationale and supporting evidence for their decision.\nL45:     3. Canadian Good means:\nL46:        * a good wholly manufactured or originating in Canada; or, a product containing imported components that has undergone sufficient change in Canada in a manner that satisfies the definition specified under the Canada-United-States-Mexico Agreement (CUSMA) Rules of Origin. For the purposes of this definition, the reference to \"territory of the Parties\" in the CUSMA (Canada-United-States-Mexico Agreement) Rules of Origin is to be replaced with \"Canada\".\nL47:     4. Canadian Service means:\nL48:        * a service wholly provided by (natural persons/individuals) based in Canada.\nL49:        * services include construction services.\nL50:     5. Canadian Value-Added means:\nL51:        * in relation to services, a Canadian Service or the proportion of the service contract performed by natural persons based in Canada; and\nL52:        * in relation to goods, a Canadian Good or the value of the portion of the good produced in Canada or the difference between the dutiable value of the imported goods and the selling price, taking into account any value added by manufacturers and distributors, and including any costs incurred in Canada related to:\nL53:          * research and development,\nL54:          * sales and marketing,\nL55:          * communications and manuals,\nL56:          * customization, modifications, installation, and support,\nL57:          * warehousing, distribution, and logistics,\nL58:          * training and after-sales service.\nL59:     6. Canadian Content means Canadian Value-Added\nL60:     7. Applicable trading partner means:\nL61:        * a country or customs territory listed in the cite5†schedule to the Canadian International Trade Tribunal Procurement Inquiry Regulations†laws-lois.justice.gc.ca as a party to one or more of the trade agreements that applies to the procurement process.\nL62:     8. Supplier of an applicable trading partner means:\nL63:        * a supplier that has a place of business in an Applicable trading partner where it conducts activities on a permanent basis that is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours; or,\nL64:        * a joint venture where at least one member of the joint venture has a place of business in an Applicable trading partner where it conducts activities on a permanent basis that is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours, and all other members of the joint venture either meet this requirement or are a Canadian supplier.\nL65:   4. ## 4. Application\nL66:     1. The Policy is issued pursuant to the cite6†Buy Canadian Procurement Policy Framework .\nL67:     2. The Policy supersedes the Interim Policy on Reciprocal Procurement in cases where both apply.\nL68:     3. The Policy supersedes the Canadian Content Policy in cases where both apply.\nL69:     4. Financial thresholds are introduced in a phased approach and reflect the total estimated value of the procurement inclusive of all applicable taxes. The Policy applies to all competitive Strategic Procurement where the financial threshold is met:\nL70:       1. Effective December 16, 2025: all Strategic Procurement $25M and up published after that date; and\nL71:       2. Effective June 15, 2026: all Strategic Procurement $5M and up published after that date.\nL72:     5. Canada reserves the right to apply the requirements of the Policy to procurements that are otherwise excluded from application, at its sole discretion. Departments and agencies are strongly encouraged to apply the requirements of the Policy to excluded procurements wherever feasible, even below applicable thresholds, to support domestic industry.\nL73:     6. On a case-by-case basis where strictly necessary, departments and agencies may exclude procurements otherwise subject to the Policy where one or more of the following exceptions applies:\nL74:       1. Best value – where application of the Policy is expected to result in a cost increase of 25% or more above current market rates, such pricing may be deemed unreasonable or inconsistent with sound stewardship, and may not represent best value for Canada;\nL75:       2. Public Interest – where the application of the Policy would otherwise be inconsistent with the public interest;\nL76:       3. No capacity or availability – where the Canadian good is not produced in Canada in sufficient and reasonably available quantities and/or of a satisfactory quality;\nL77:       4. Undue Delay – where the application of the Policy would result in delays to the procurement of critical defence or national security equipment, which could negatively impact Canadian defence or national security operations;\nL78:       5. Commercial Off-The-Shelf (COTS) and Military Off-The-Shelf (MOTS) Products.\nL79:     7. Use of the exceptions listed at section 4.6 must be approved by the minister, of the department or agency for which the procurement is being undertaken.\nL80:       1. Where the procurement is conducted by a common service provider (e.g., Public Services and Procurement Canada, Shared Services Canada), the minister responsible for the common service provider must also approve the invocation of any exception prior to solicitation. This requirement applies regardless of whether the exception is initiated by the client department.\nL81:       2. For procurements that are issued by a common service provider on behalf of multiple departments or as part of a government-wide procurement instrument (e.g., National Master Standing Offers), the minister responsible for the common service provider may approve the use of the exceptions under section 4.6 without requiring approval from individual client departments.\nL82: In such cases, the common service provider must ensure that the rationale and supporting documentation reflect the scope and impact of the exception across all affected departments.\nL83:   5. ## 5. Policy Requirements\nL84:     1. Departments and agencies of the Government of Canada are to implement the requirements of the Policy in their procurement processes. To do so, contracting authorities in collaboration with the business owners are required to:\nL85:       1. Determine if the Policy applies to the procurement in question, pursuant to sections 4.4 to 4.7; and,\nL86:       2. Prior to issuing the tender documents, the contracting authority must, if the procurement is subject to the Policy, incorporate the defined terms and associated Canadian Content requirements, outlined in the Policy.\nL87:     2. Canadian Supplier\nL88:     3. This requirement seeks to support Canadian industries and ensure that Canadian suppliers are prioritized in procurement processes.\nL89:       1. When the Policy applies and no exception is applicable, competition is open to Canadian suppliers and, if any international trade agreements apply to the solicitation, Suppliers of an applicable trading partner;\nL90:       2. Offers from eligible Canadian suppliers, as defined in section 3.2, will receive a 10 percent reduction to the total value of their financial proposal only for the purpose of the evaluation and it will not impact the actual offer price (see cite7†Appendix D ).\nL91:       3. Supplier Eligibility Attestation\nL92:       4. In order to be considered for the purposes of section 5.2.1, offerors must complete an attestation confirming that they meet the requirements of either definition as part of their offer as outlined in cite8†Appendix B .\nL93:     4. Canadian Content\nL94:     5. This requirement seeks to support Canadian industries and ensure that Canadian content is prioritized in the procurement process.\nL95:       1. Selection Methodologies\nL96:       2. When establishing the requirement of the solicitation, the contracting authority must determine which of the two following methodologies shall be applied for offer evaluation:\nL97:         1. Point-Rated Methodology\nL98:         2. Allocate 25 percent of the total evaluation score to the Canadian Value-Added requirement criterion (see cite9†Appendix E ).\nL99:         3. Mandatory Canadian Materials (as defined under the Policy on Prioritizing Canadian Materials) must be excluded from this calculation.\nL100:         4. Price-Based Methodology\nL101:         5. Where point-rated criteria methodology may not be suitable, use a price-based evaluation credit 25 percent applied to financial proposal at evaluation. This is only for the purpose of the evaluation and will not impact the actual offer price (see cite10†Appendix F ).\nL102:         6. Mandatory Canadian Materials (as defined under the Policy on Prioritizing Canadian Materials in Federal Procurements) must be excluded from this calculation.\nL103:       3. Mandatory Minimum\nL104:       4. The contracting authority in collaboration with the business owner can set a minimum mandatory Canadian Value-Added requirement as part of the evaluation criteria only where justified by procurement objectives and approved as per section 5.7.\nL105:       5. Applicable Canadian Content Attestation\nL106:       6. When applicable, offerors must complete an attestation confirming their commitment to use Canadian goods and/or Canadian services and/or Canadian value-added in the performance of the contract requirements as outlined in cite11†Appendix C .\nL107:     6. Contracting authorities must evaluate all offers in accordance with the requirements set forth in sections 5.2 and 5.3.\nL108:     7. If during the performance of the contract, due to changes in market availability or other substantiated factors, the contractor determines that it is no longer feasible to meet its Canadian content requirement, they may submit a written request to the contracting authority. The request must include detailed justification and supporting documentation.\nL109:       1. Approval of any such request is subject to the authorization of the client department’s minister, and, where the procurement is conducted by a common service provider (e.g., Public Services and Procurement Canada, Shared Services Canada), the minister responsible for the common service provider, as stipulated in section 4.7 of this Policy.\nL110:       2. Departments must ensure that contractors continue to meet all contractual obligations including delivery, quality, and performance requirements, while approval is pending. Approval delays do not relieve the contractor of these obligations. If the request is approved, departments must honour valid claims for payment in accordance with the amended terms.\nL111:       3. The contracting authority may determine that a price adjustment is warranted to offset the difference in Canadian content value. Any such adjustment must be reasonable and informed by sufficient documentation. In the absence of sufficient documentation provided by the contractor, the decision to adjust pricing or contract terms will be at the sole discretion of the contracting authority.\nL112:     8. Ensure that accurate and complete procurement records are created and maintained in the procurement file.\nL113:     9. Conditions for Revised Evaluation Criteria, and Selection Methodologies\nL114:     10. Any changes or revisions to requirements as set forth in sections 5.2 and 5.3 are subject to approvals at the Assistant Deputy Minister level. This includes any adjustments to the reduction to the total value of the financial proposal for Canadian Supplier and modifications to point-rated or price-based methodologies for Canadian Content.\nL115:     11. Documented Exceptions\nL116:     12. Approved exceptions must be fully documented by the contracting authority in the procurement file, including the rationale, supporting evidence, and required approvals.\nL117:   6. ## 6. Roles and Responsibilities\nL118:     1. Public Services and Procurement Canada (PSPC (Public Services and Procurement Canada)) is responsible for:\nL119:       1. Developing and issuing supporting policy instruments, guidance materials, and tools to ensure consistent interpretation and application of the various Canadian priority measures.\nL120:     2. Common Service Providers (PSPC (Public Services and Procurement Canada) and Shared Services Canada) are responsible for:\nL121:       1. For procurements managed as a common service provider, implementing mechanisms to monitor compliance, tracking performance, and assessing the effectiveness of Canadian priority requirements of procurements under its responsibility.\nL122:     3. Departments and Agencies are responsible for:\nL123:       1. Determining applicability of the Policy to each procurement;\nL124:       2. Ensuring Statements of Work and specifications support Canadian suppliers and Canadian content; and\nL125:       3. Obtaining and validating (when required) offeror declarations and attestations.\nL126:   7. ## 7. Monitoring and Reporting\nL127:     1. Departments and agencies must:\nL128:       1. Monitor contractor compliance throughout the procurement lifecycle;\nL129:       2. Ensure that accurate and complete procurement records are created and maintained to support verification of compliance, including offeror declarations and attestations, and any exceptions where applicable;\nL130:       3. Support any government-wide data reporting obligations under the Buy Canadian Procurement Framework; and\nL131:       4. Maintain records of offeror declarations and attestations, and any deviations from contractual obligations.\nL132:   8. ## 8. Enquiries\nL133:     1. Please direct enquiries about the Policy to the organizational unit in your department responsible for this subject matter. For interpretation of the Policy, the responsible organizational unit should contact: spac.paachetezcanadien-apbuycanadian.pspc@tpsgc-pwgsc.gc.ca.\nL134: ## Appendix A: Strategic Procurement – List of Sectors and Commodities\nL135: \nL136: ### 1. Defence and Security\nL137: \nL138: Goods and services that support Canada’s national defence, military readiness, and security operations, including systems and capabilities essential for sovereignty and protection.\nL139: \nL140: Examples include, but are not limited to: weapons, vehicles, aircraft, ships, naval systems, electronic warfare, logistics, training, security services\nL141: ### 2. Health and Pharmaceutical\nL142: \nL143: Goods and services related to healthcare delivery, medical treatment, and to ensure public health resilience and emergency preparedness and response.\nL144: \nL145: Examples include, but are not limited to: medical and surgical equipment, healthcare services, nursing care, medical waste, drugs, vaccines and biologicals\nL146: ### 3. Infrastructure, Construction, and Transportation\nL147: \nL148: Physical assets and systems essential for public and economic activity; services and materials involved in constructing, renovating, or demolishing buildings and structures; and goods and services enabling the movement of people and property.\nL149: \nL150: Examples include, but are not limited to: buildings, transportation infrastructure, mobility, utilities, environmental cleanup, facilities management, aerospace, shipbuilding, motor vehicles\nL151: ### 4. Information and Communications Technology (ICT)\nL152: \nL153: Digital systems, hardware, software, network infrastructure, and related services that enable government functions.\nL154: \nL155: Examples include, but are not limited to: digital infrastructure, data centers, cloud, networks, telecommunications, 5G, connectivity, software and hardware\nL156: ### 5. Consumer and Industrial Goods and Materials\nL157: \nL158: Commercial products and materials intended for personal use and consumption or industrial production and operations.\nL159: \nL160: Examples include, but are not limited to: clothing, textiles, furniture, fuel, chemicals and plastics, scientific and testing equipment, fire suppression, machinery.\nL161: \nL162: \nL163: \nL164: Professional services directly supporting the above-listed sectors and commodities are also included.\nL165: \nL166: ## Appendix B: Supplier Eligibility Attestation Form\nL167: ### Part I - Submission Eligibility - Solicitation of Offer\nL168: \nL169: This solicitation of offers is open to Canadian suppliers and, if any international trade agreements apply to the solicitation, Suppliers of an applicable trading partner.\nL170: \nL171: Submissions received from suppliers of non-trading partner countries will be deemed ineligible and will be rejected without further consideration.\nL172: ### Instructions to Buyers:\nL173: \nL174:   * Include only the rows for the trade agreements that apply to the solicitation of offers and delete rows for any trade agreements that do not apply.\nL175:   * If no trade agreements apply to the requirement, do not include the table below.\nL176: Applicable Trade Agreement(s)\nL177: ---\nL178: World Trade Organization Agreement on Government Procurement (WTO-GPA)\nL179: Canada-Chile Free Trade Agreement\nL180: Canada-Colombia Free Trade Agreement\nL181: Canada-European Union Comprehensive and Economic Agreement (CETA)\nL182: Canada-Honduras Free Trade Agreement\nL183: Canada-Korea Free Trade Agreement\nL184: Canada-Panama Free Trade Agreement\nL185: Canada-Peru Free Trade Agreement\nL186: Canada-Ukraine Free Trade Agreement\nL187: Canada-United Kingdom Trade Continuity Agreement (CUKTCA)\nL188: Canadian Free Trade Agreement (CFTA)\nL189: Comprehensive and Progressive Agreement for Trans-Pacific Partnership (CPTPP)\nL190: ### Part II - Eligibility - Canadian Supplier\nL191: \nL192: The Policy on Prioritizing Canadian Suppliers and Canadian Content in Strategic Federal Procurements (the Policy) seeks to support Canadian industries and ensure that Canadian suppliers are prioritized in procurement processes.\nL193: \nL194: To receive priority treatment as a Canadian supplier, offerors must meet the definition of Canadian supplier as set out under the Policy.\nL195: \nL196: Applicable trading partner means:\nL197:   * a country or customs territory listed in the cite5†schedule to the Canadian International Trade Tribunal Procurement Inquiry Regulations†laws-lois.justice.gc.ca as a party to one or more of the trade agreements that applies to the procurement process.\nL198: \nL199: Canadian supplier means:\nL200:   * a supplier that has a place of business in Canada where it conducts activities on a permanent basis which is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours; or, a joint venture where each member of the joint venture has a place of business in Canada where it conducts activities on a permanent basis that is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours; and\nL201:     * is registered and files taxes in Canada (e.g., GST (Goods and Services Tax)/HST (Harmonized Sales Tax), corporate income tax);\nL202:     * maintains a registered address in Canada and employs personnel and/or conducts day-to-day business activities in Canada; and will not subcontract work to non-Canadian suppliers or individuals located outside Canada, in a manner that results in minimal value-added activities being performed within Canada.\nL203: Supplier of an applicable trading partner means:\nL204:   * a supplier that has a place of business in an Applicable trading partner where it conducts activities on a permanent basis that is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours; or\nL205:   * a joint venture where at least one member of the joint venture has a place of business in an Applicable trading partner where it conducts activities on a permanent basis that is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours, and all other members of the joint venture either meet this requirement or are a Canadian supplier.\nL206: Offerors must submit this Supplier Eligibility Attestation Form at offer closing.\nL207: ### Part III - Instructions\nL208: \nL209: To confirm and attest your eligibility as a Canadian supplier or as a supplier of applicable trading partners, please carefully review and complete this form.\nL210: ## Supplier Eligibility Attestation Form\nL211: \nL212: The Offeror,                Enter Legal Name of Offeror              , is a Canadian supplier, as defined in the Policy on Prioritizing Canadian Suppliers and Canadian Content in Strategic Federal Procurements.\nL213: \nL214: The address, as provided below, is the Offeror’s place of business in Canada where:\nL215:   * business activities are conducted on a permanent basis,\nL216:   * can be accessed during normal business hours,\nL217:   * can be clearly identified by business name,\nL218:   * is registered and files taxes in Canada (e.g., GST (Goods and Services Tax)/HST (Harmonized Sales Tax), corporate income tax),\nL219:   * employs personnel and/or conducts day-to-day business activities in Canada.\nL220: In the case of a joint venture, where each member of the joint venture has a place of business in Canada where it conducts activities on a permanent basis that is clearly identified by name and accessible during normal business hours.\nL221: \nL222: \nL223: \nL224: Address(es) of Place of Business  | Normal Business Hours\nL225: _____________________________  | _____________________________\nL226: Operating Business Name  | Legal Business Name\nL227: _____________________________  | _____________________________\nL228: For the purpose of determining eligibility for priority treatment under the Policy, Canada will only use the address(es) provided in this attestation.\nL229: \nL230: \nL231: \nL232: OR\nL233: \nL234: \nL235: \nL236: The Offeror,                Enter Legal Name of Offeror              , is a supplier of an applicable trading partner, as defined in Part I. The address provided below is the Offeror’s permanent place of business, or in the case of a joint venture, the address(es) provided below are the places of business of each member of the joint venture.\nL237: Insert /Address(es) of Places of Business\nL238: _____________________________\nL239: \nL240: \nL241: \nL242: \nL243: \nL244: By signing this attestation, the Offeror acknowledges, agrees, and attests to the following:\nL245:   * The Offeror attests that they will remain in full compliance with the statements made in this attestation throughout the offer period and throughout the duration of any resulting contract.\nL246:   * The Offeror attests that evidence supporting their eligibility is available and agrees to provide any supporting documentation requested for verification purposes, at any time, including during reviews, in response to complaints, or after contract award.\nL247:   * The Offeror acknowledges that the contracting authority may, based on supporting evidence and consideration of all relevant facts and circumstances, determine that the Offeror does not meet the definition of a Canadian supplier for the purposes of the Policy.\nL248:   * The Offeror acknowledges that the discovery of false or misleading information, or failure to provide requested evidence, may result in contractual remedies including, but not limited to:\nL249:     * Offer disqualification;\nL250:     * Liquidated damages;\nL251:     * Ineligibility for contract award;\nL252:     * Retention of deposits or holdbacks;\nL253:     * Termination of contract, setting aside of standing offers or suspension or cancellation of supply arrangements;\nL254:     * Disqualification under the Ineligibility and Suspension Policy.\nL255: \nL256: \nL257: Offeror’s Signature  | Date\nL258: _____________________________  | _____________________________\nL259: ## Appendix C: Canadian Content Attestation Form\nL260: The Policy on Prioritizing Canadian Suppliers and Canadian Content in Strategic Federal Procurements (the Policy) seeks to support Canadian industries and ensure that Canadian content is prioritized in the procurement process. This is achieved by granting additional scores in offer evaluation for the inclusion of Canadian value-added. It may be applied in the technical evaluation as a point-rated criterion, or in the financial evaluation as a price-based evaluation credit.\nL261: In order to receive additional scores for the inclusion of Canadian value-added, the goods and services supplied must meet the Policy definition of “Canadian value-added”.\nL262: \nL263: Canadian Good means:\nL264:   * a good wholly manufactured or originating in Canada; or a product containing imported components that has undergone sufficient change in Canada in a manner that satisfies the definition specified under the Canada-United-States-Mexico Agreement (CUSMA) Rules of Origin. For the purposes of this definition, the reference to \"territory of the Parties\" in the CUSMA (Canada-United-States-Mexico Agreement) Rules of Origin is to be replaced with \"Canada\".\nL265: \nL266: \nL267: \nL268: Canadian Service means:\nL269:   * a service wholly provided by (natural persons/individuals) based in Canada.\nL270:   * Services include construction services.\nL271: \nL272: \nL273: \nL274: Canadian Value-Added means:\nL275:   * in relation to services, a Canadian service or the proportion of the service contract performed by natural persons based in Canada; and\nL276:   * in relation to goods, a Canadian good or the value of the portion of the good produced in Canada or the difference between the dutiable value of the imported goods and the selling price, taking into account any value added by manufacturers and distributors, and including any costs incurred in Canada related to:\nL277:     * research and development,\nL278:     * sales and marketing,\nL279:     * communications and manuals,\nL280:     * customization, modifications, installation, and support,\nL281:     * warehousing, distribution, and logistics,\nL282:     * training and after-sales service.\nL283: \nL284: \nL285: Canadian Content means Canadian Value-Added\nL286: \nL287: The total Canadian value-added in the offer must be provided by the offerors, identifying the value for each line item in their submission.\nL288: \nL289: Offerors must submit this Canadian Content Attestation form at offer closing. Failure to submit this form by offer closing will result in the offerors waiving their right to receive additional scores during offer evaluation.\nL290: To confirm and attest the total Canadian value-added included in your offer proposal, please carefully review and complete this form.\nL291: ## Canadian Content Attestation Form\nL292: \nL293: (For example purposes – can be adjusted based on the nature of the procurement)\nL294: \nL295: \nL296: \nL297: By completing and submitting this form to the contracting authority,\nL298: \nL299: the offeror                Enter Legal Name of Offeror               attests that all information provided is true, accurate, and complete.\nL300: \nL301: \nL302: \nL303: For all goods and/or services, please provide a line-item breakdown of the offer proposal and indicate the corresponding Canadian value-added (in CAD value) for each item.\nL304: Line Item of goods and/or services  | Canadian Value-Added (CAD)\nL305: --- | ---\nL306:    |\nL307:    |\nL308:    |\nL309:    |\nL310:    |\nL311:    |\nL312:    |\nL313: Total Canadian Value-Added in the offer  |\nL314: \nL315: \nL316: \nL317: By signing this attestation, the offeror acknowledges, agrees, and attests to the following:\nL318:   * The offeror attests that they will remain in full compliance with the statements made in this attestation throughout the offer period and any resulting contract.\nL319:   * The offeror attests that evidence regarding the origin of goods and/or services is available and agrees to provide any supporting documentation requested for verification purposes, at any time, including during reviews, in response to complaints, or after contract award.\nL320:   * The offeror acknowledges that the contracting authority may, based on supporting evidence and consideration of all relevant facts and circumstances, determine that the goods or services do not meet the definition of Canadian value-added for the purposes of the Policy.\nL321:   * The offeror acknowledges that the discovery of false or misleading information, or failure to provide requested evidence, may result in contractual remedies including, but not limited to:\nL322:     * Offer disqualification;\nL323:     * Liquidated damages;\nL324:     * Ineligibility for contract award;\nL325:     * Retention of deposits or holdbacks;\nL326:     * Termination of contract or setting aside of standing offers, or suspension or cancellation of supply arrangements;\nL327:     * Disqualification under the Ineligibility and Suspension Policy.\nL328: \nL329: \nL330: Offeror’s Signature  | Date\nL331: _____________________________  | _____________________________\nL332: ## Appendix D: Canadian Supplier Priority – Financial Evaluation\nL333: \nL334: \nL335: \nL336: When compliant offers are received from Canadian suppliers, a 10 percent reduction to the total value of each Canadian Supplier’s financial proposal will be applied.\nL337: \nL338: \nL339: \nL340: The following formula will be used:\nL341: ### FORMULA:\nL342: \nL343: $\\text{Evaluated Price}=\\text{Offer Price}\\bullet (1-0.10)$\nL344: \nL345: The Offer Price is the total value of the financial proposal submitted by the Canadian Supplier.\nL346: \nL347: 0.10 is the 10 percent notional evaluation reduction factor.\nL348: \nL349: The Evaluated Price will be used for evaluation purposes only (does not affect actual offer price).\nL350: ### EXAMPLE\nL351: \nL352: An offeror submitted an offer with a total value of $100,000 $\\text{Evaluated Price}=\\mathrm{\\$\\; 100\\; 000}\\bullet (1-0.10)$ $\\text{Evaluated Price}=\\mathrm{\\$\\; 100\\; 000}\\bullet 0.90=\\mathrm{90\\; 000}$\nL353: \nL354: For evaluation purposes, the offer price would be considered, for purposes of evaluation, as $90,000, while the actual offer price remains $100,000.\nL355: ## Appendix E: Canadian Value-Added Evaluation Methodology and Scoring\nL356: \nL357: In cases where point-rated criteria methodology is exercised, the following calculation should be used.\nL358: Typically, solicitation of offers include, among other things, technical and financial evaluation criteria. In this example, technical evaluation, pricing evaluation and Canadian value-added (CVA) evaluation criteria will be included. The sum of all three weightings (technical, pricing and CVA (Canadian value-added) ) is to equal 100 percent.\nL359: The CVA (Canadian value-added) Weighting is 25% of the total evaluation score allocated to CVA (Canadian value-added). This means that the weightings of the technical and financial portions of the evaluation is 75 points out of 100.\nL360: \nL361: For example, if the percent allotted to the technical merit score is equal 35, the percent for the pricing score would be 40. The weightings in that case would be:\nL362: CVA (Canadian value-added) evaluation weighting:  | 25%\nL363: Technical evaluation weighting:  | 35%\nL364: Financial evaluation weighting:  | 40%\nL365: TOTAL:  | 100%\nL366: ### FORMULA:\nL367: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBuy Canadian update: strategic procurement threshold lowered to $5 million | CanadaBuys (https://canadabuys.canada.ca/en/news-and-events/buy-canadian-update-strategic-procurement-threshold-lowered)\nciteturn32view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://canadabuys.canada.ca/en/news-and-events/buy-canadian-update-strategic-procurement-threshold-lowered\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 65\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAccess to Information Act (https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/A-1/section-86.html?wbdisable=true)\nciteturn32view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/A-1/section-86.html?wbdisable=true\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContracting Policy Notice 2025-1: Amendments to the Government Contracts Regulations - Canada.ca (https://www.canada.ca/en/treasury-board-secretariat/services/policy-notice/2025-1.html)\nciteturn32view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.canada.ca/en/treasury-board-secretariat/services/policy-notice/2025-1.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 103\n"
